README-KSH -- the Korn shell, and what it needs from the emulator================================================================`ksh -c '<commands>'` has always worked here completely: for loops, variableassignment and expansion, $PWD, the lot.  Typing at it did nothing -- noprompt, no command ever ran.  That was never a fault in ksh.WHAT WAS HAPPENING------------------pdksh reads a command line with    c = read(ttyfd, line, LINE);        SRC/pdksh/sh/lex.c, and LINE is 256Microware's OS-9/68000 Technical Reference says of I$Read that d1.l is aMAXIMUM, not a count to wait for:    "The number of bytes requested is read unless: the end-of-file is     reached; an end-of-record occurs (SCF only); an error condition occurs."    "SCF devices usually terminate the read when a carriage return is     reached."os9exec was not applying the end-of-record character on that path, so a256-byte read waited for 256 bytes.  Nobody types 256 characters, so the readnever returned and ksh never saw a command.  `bash' and `sh' were unaffectedbecause they use I$ReadLn, which is a different call.Two things were checked and are NOT the cause: isatty() works, so ksh doesknow it is interactive; and the line editor is not to blame -- turning it offwith `set +o emacs' makes the PROMPT appear and the command read still hangs.WITH THE EMULATOR CORRECTED---------------------------ksh is a complete interactive shell:    # echo hello from ksh    hello from ksh    # for i in 1 2 3; do echo loop $i; done    loop 1    loop 2    loop 3    # x=42; echo x is $x    x is 42    # exit`expreserve' -- vi's crash-recovery helper -- is fixed by the same thing.  Itasks for 388 bytes from the terminal and had the same problem.Those two are the only programs on this disk affected.  Every program herethat reads a terminal was checked: 60 do, and all but three ask for one byteat a time, which was never affected.  The third, `flex', asks for 8192 butdoes not reach that read interactively.IF YOUR os9exec STILL HAS THE OLD BEHAVIOUR-------------------------------------------Use `bash' or `sh' interactively -- both are here and both work -- and use`ksh -c' for anything you want ksh's syntax for.  The details, with aminimal reproduction in 68000 assembly, are in the collection's notes.